# ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
#  Lead capture runs SERVER-SIDE, through /api/lead/.
#
#  Nothing in this file is required for the site to build or for the form to
#  work — every value has a safe fallback. Setting them is how you harden it.
#
#  Check what a running deployment actually has with:  GET /api/health/
# ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════

# ── Supabase (the WarriorCRM intake queue) ───────────────────────────────
# NOTE THE MISSING `NEXT_PUBLIC_` PREFIX. That prefix is what tells Next a
# variable may be inlined into the JavaScript sent to the browser, so leaving it
# off is the mechanism that keeps the key server-only. Never add it to these.
#
# SUPABASE_SERVICE_ROLE_KEY is the goal: with it, this server is the only writer,
# so you can revoke the anon INSERT policy on `public_leads` and close the queue
# to the public entirely. Without it the server falls back to the publishable
# key — still safe from the browser's point of view (the browser never sees any
# key now), but the anon policy has to stay open.
SUPABASE_URL=https://YOUR-PROJECT.supabase.co
SUPABASE_SERVICE_ROLE_KEY=your-service-role-key

# Optional fallback if you are not ready to issue a service-role key.
# SUPABASE_ANON_KEY=your-supabase-anon-key

# ── Cloudflare Turnstile (bot protection) ────────────────────────────────
# Both halves are needed. The site key renders the widget; the secret is what
# lets the server verify the token with Cloudflare — a widget without server
# verification stops nothing, because a bot just calls the API directly.
#
# While TURNSTILE_SECRET_KEY is unset, the endpoint accepts submissions without
# a challenge and the rate limiter plus honeypot are the only bot defences.
# NEXT_PUBLIC_TURNSTILE_SITE_KEY=0x4AAAAAAA...
# TURNSTILE_SECRET_KEY=0x4AAAAAAA...

# ── Rate limiting (optional tuning) ──────────────────────────────────────
# Two tiers. `BURST` counts every request and stops hammering; `RATE_LIMIT_MAX`
# counts only accepted, non-duplicate leads and caps how many rows one address
# can create. Splitting them means a visitor who mistypes their phone number
# repeatedly is never locked out.
#
# State is per Node process: a restart clears it, and multiple instances each
# get their own allowance.
# LEAD_BURST_LIMIT_MAX=30
# LEAD_RATE_LIMIT_MAX=5
# LEAD_RATE_LIMIT_WINDOW_MS=600000
# LEAD_MAX_BODY_BYTES=16384
# LEAD_DEDUPE_WINDOW_MS=300000

# ── Extra origins allowed to POST /api/lead/ ─────────────────────────────
# Comma-separated. Same-origin is always allowed; add hosts only if another site
# should submit into this endpoint.
# LEAD_ALLOWED_ORIGINS=https://spartacusmartialarts.com

# ── Site URL ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Canonical origin used for sitemap, robots, RSS and og: tags. Defaults to the
# production domain, so you only set this for previews/staging.
# NEXT_PUBLIC_SITE_URL=https://kishorekumarcoach.com

# ── Where these live ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Local dev:  copy this file to `.env.local`.
# Production: Hostinger runs the app from Git, so set them in hPanel's Node
#             application environment — NOT in this repo.
# CI builds:  GitHub → Settings → Secrets and variables → Actions. CI only needs
#             them if you want the build to bake in NEXT_PUBLIC_* values; the
#             server-side ones are read at runtime, not build time.
